\label{cor:expansion-depth}
Let $\lambda_{\max}(J_n, t_n) := \max_{\boldsymbol{x} \in J_n} w(\boldsymbol{x})$ denote the maximum total exit rate in the active set, and set $\mu_n := \lambda_{\max}(J_n,t_n)\,\Delta t_n$.
In a CTMC with all exit rates bounded above by $\lambda_{\max}$, the holding time at each state is at least $\mathrm{Exp}(\lambda_{\max})$, so the number of reactions along any trajectory in time $s$ is stochastically dominated by $\mathrm{Poisson}(\lambda_{\max}\,s)$.
A state at graph distance $d$ from $J_n$ therefore requires at least $d$ such events to be reached.
After expanding $J_n$ by depth $d$, the expansion-induced boundary outflux satisfies
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:expansion-outflux-bound}
\int_{t_n}^{t_{n+1}} \Phi_{\mathrm{out}}(s)\, ds
\;\leq\;
\mu_n \cdot \mathrm{P}\!\bigl(\mathrm{Poisson}(\mu_n) \geq d\bigr).
\end{equation}
For $d=1$: $\mathrm{P}(\mathrm{Poisson}(\mu_n)\geq 1) = 1-e^{-\mu_n} \leq \mu_n$, so the bound reduces to $\mu_n^2$.
The condition $\mu_n \le d$ is equivalent to $\Delta t_n \le d/\lambda_{\max}$; for systems with $\kappa_n = \lambda_{\max}/\Phi_{\mathrm{total}} \le d/\varepsilon_{\Delta t}$, the flux step $\Delta t_n = \varepsilon_{\Delta t}/\Phi_{\mathrm{total}}$ already satisfies this, so depth $d$ is guaranteed without any additional cap. For slow-start systems where $\Phi_{\mathrm{total}} \to 0$, an explicit cap is required (Remark~\ref{rem:max-propensity-cap}).
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Let $N(s)$ denote the number of reactions in $[0,s]$. Since each holding time is $\geq\mathrm{Exp}(\lambda_{\max})$, $N(s)\preceq\mathrm{Poisson}(\lambda_{\max}s)$ stochastically.
All mass starts in $J_n$; reaching $J'$ requires $N(s)\geq d$, so
$\Phi_{\mathrm{out}}(t_n+s)\leq\lambda_{\max}\,\mathrm{P}(N(s)\geq d)\leq\lambda_{\max}\,\mathrm{P}(\mathrm{Poisson}(\lambda_{\max}s)\geq d)$.
Integrating and applying monotonicity of the Poisson survivor in $s$ gives~\eqref{eq:expansion-outflux-bound}.
For $d=1$: $\mathrm{P}(\mathrm{Poisson}(\mu_n)\geq 1)=1-e^{-\mu_n}\leq\mu_n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Expansion strategy and the depth-one regime]
\label{rem:expansion-strategy}
Corollary~\ref{cor:expansion-depth} shows that depth $d=1$ suffices whenever $\mu_n\leq 1$.  For highly stiff systems this is enforced by the max-propensity cap (Remark~\ref{rem:max-propensity-cap}).  Within the depth-one regime two practical expansion strategies are available:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Stoichiometric expansion} adds every state reachable by a single reaction step from any active state, covering all $R$ reaction channels simultaneously.  This is the conservative choice, appropriate when $\kappa_n>1$ (multiple fast channels) or when the dominant reaction direction is not known \textit{a priori}.
  \item \textbf{SSA expansion} samples one next-reaction direction per active state according to the propensity distribution, adding only the most probable single-step neighbor.  When $\kappa_n\approx 1$ (a single reaction channel dominates locally), SSA implements $d=1$ with substantially lower state-space growth: stoichiometric expansion inflates $|S_n|$ by up to factor $R$ per step, while SSA adds exactly one new state per active state.
\end{itemize}
In systems where $\kappa_n$ varies across the state space---one dominant channel locally but multiple channels relevant globally---stoichiometric expansion offers safety without requiring prior knowledge of which channel will dominate.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Consistent parameter selection via spatial stiffness]
\label{rem:consistent-params}
Corollaries~\ref{cor:local-error-pruning}, \ref{cor:flux-timestep}, and~\ref{cor:expansion-depth} and Remark~\ref{rem:expansion-strategy} provide a self-consistent framework for parameter selection, governed by the spatial stiffness ratio $\kappa=\lambda_{\max}/\Phi_{\mathrm{total}}$:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Time step:} use the total-flux rule $\Delta t = \varepsilon_{\Delta t}/\Phi_{\mathrm{total}}$ (leading-order error $\lesssim\varepsilon_{\Delta t}$, $\mu=\kappa\,\varepsilon_{\Delta t}$); for highly stiff systems apply the max-propensity cap (Remark~\ref{rem:max-propensity-cap}) to enforce $\mu\leq 1$ unconditionally \hfill (Corollary~\ref{cor:flux-timestep}),
  \item \textbf{Expansion depth and strategy:} use $d=1$ (ensured by $\kappa\,\varepsilon_{\Delta t}\leq 1$ or the cap); choose SSA expansion when $\kappa\approx 1$ (one dominant channel) and stoichiometric expansion when $\kappa>1$ \hfill (Corollary~\ref{cor:expansion-depth}, Remark~\ref{rem:expansion-strategy}),
  \item \textbf{Flux tolerance:} $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{flux}} \ll \varepsilon_{\Delta t}$, e.g., $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{flux}} = \varepsilon_{\Delta t}/w_{\max}$ \hfill (Corollary~\ref{cor:local-error-pruning}).
\end{itemize}
\end{remark}

