\begin{remark}[Total flux versus boundary outflux]
\label{rem:total-vs-boundary-flux}
One might consider protecting states based on their \emph{boundary outflux}
$\Phi_{\partial}(\boldsymbol{x},t):=p(\boldsymbol{x},t)\sum_{\boldsymbol{y}\in J'}\mathbf{A}_{\boldsymbol{y}\boldsymbol{x}}$
---the rate at which probability leaves $\boldsymbol{x}$ to states outside the current active set---rather than total flux $\Phi(\boldsymbol{x},t)=p(\boldsymbol{x},t)w(\boldsymbol{x})$.
Total flux is preferable for two reasons.
First, genuine bottleneck states send their transitions primarily to high-probability metastable states already in $J$, so their boundary outflux is approximately zero; using boundary flux would \emph{fail} to protect them.
Total flux $p(s_b)w(s_b)$ remains large because $w(s_b)$ is large: it measures the rate at which probability \emph{cycles through} $s_b$ regardless of where those transitions lead, which is precisely the signature of a fast-transit connector.
Second, computing $\Phi_{\partial}$ requires knowing $J'$, which is determined by the pruning decision itself, creating a circular dependency. Total flux $p(\boldsymbol{x})\cdot(-\mathbf{A}_{\boldsymbol{x}\boldsymbol{x}})$ is a byproduct of the generator diagonal, available without any knowledge of the pruning outcome.
\end{remark}

