\begin{remark}[Pruning uses total flux, not boundary outflux]
\label{rem:total-vs-boundary-flux}
Remark~\ref{rem:phiout-central} identifies $\Phi_{\mathrm{out}}$ as the right quantity for time-stepping.  For \emph{pruning}, however, the per-state boundary outflux $\Phi_{\partial}(\boldsymbol{x},t):=p(\boldsymbol{x},t)\sum_{\boldsymbol{y}\in J'}\mathbf{A}_{\boldsymbol{y}\boldsymbol{x}}$ is the \emph{wrong} protection criterion.  Genuine bottleneck states send most transitions to high-probability metastable states already in $J$, so $\Phi_{\partial}(s_b) \approx 0$; the boundary-outflux criterion would \emph{fail} to protect them.  Total flux $\Phi(s_b) = p(s_b)w(s_b)$ remains large because $w(s_b)$ is large---it measures probability cycling through $s_b$ regardless of destination, which is precisely the signature of a fast-transit connector.  There is also a practical obstacle: $\Phi_{\partial}$ requires knowing $J'$, which depends on the pruning outcome, creating a circular dependency.  Hence \emph{pruning} protects states by total flux $\Phi(\boldsymbol{x}) = p(\boldsymbol{x})w(\boldsymbol{x})$, while \emph{time-stepping} is governed by the aggregate boundary outflux $\Phi_{\mathrm{out}}(J, t)$ of~\eqref{eq:boundary-outflux-def}.
\end{remark}

