\subsection{Adaptive Time Stepping}
By~\eqref{eq:error-is-phiout}, the per-step error satisfies $\|\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}\|_1 \lesssim \Phi_{\mathrm{out}}(J_n,t_n)\,\Delta t_n$.  Setting $\Delta t_n = \varepsilon_{\Delta t}/\Phi_{\mathrm{out}}(J_n,t_n)$ would therefore guarantee $\|\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}\|_1 \lesssim \varepsilon_{\Delta t}$ exactly.  In practice $\Phi_{\mathrm{out}}$ depends on $J_n$, which changes at each step and may be zero after stoichiometric expansion.  Since $\Phi_{\mathrm{out}} \leq \Phi_{\mathrm{total}}$ always (Definition~\ref{def:boundary-outflux}), we use $\Phi_{\mathrm{total}}$ as the computable upper bound and choose
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:flux-timestep}
\Delta t_n = \frac{\epsilon_{\Delta t}}{\Phi_{\mathrm{total}}(\mathcal{S}_n, t_n)},
\end{equation}
which guarantees $\Phi_{\mathrm{out}}\,\Delta t_n \leq \Phi_{\mathrm{total}}\,\Delta t_n = \varepsilon_{\Delta t}$ and hence $\|\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}\|_1 \lesssim \varepsilon_{\Delta t}$.  The quality of this bound depends on the expansion strategy: stoichiometric expansion drives $\Phi_{\mathrm{out}} \to 0$ (giving a second-order error bound), while flux-based expansion guarantees $\Phi_{\mathrm{out}} \leq \theta\,\Phi_{\mathrm{total}}$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:flux-expansion-bound}), tightening the bound to $\theta\,\varepsilon_{\Delta t}$.  The formula~\eqref{eq:flux-timestep} is thus the single rule that covers all expansion strategies; the expansion strategy determines the actual error-to-bound ratio $\Phi_{\mathrm{out}}/\Phi_{\mathrm{total}} \in [0,\theta]$.  The formal derivation is in Section~\ref{sec:theory}.

