\begin{remark}[Expansion strategies as boundary outflux reduction]
\label{rem:expansion-strategy}
Corollary~\ref{cor:expansion-depth} shows that depth $d=1$ suffices whenever $\mu_n \leq 1$.  Within the depth-one regime, the expansion strategy determines how aggressively $\Phi_{\mathrm{out}}$ is reduced before each step, trading state-space size against error quality:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Stoichiometric expansion} adds all $R$ stoichiometric neighbors of every active state, driving $\Phi_{\mathrm{out}} \to 0$ unconditionally.  The leading-order error vanishes and the bound in Corollary~\ref{cor:flux-timestep} tightens to $O(\varepsilon_{\Delta t}^2)$.  Cost: $|J_n|$ grows by up to factor $R$ per step, which is prohibitive when $R$ is large or propensities are oscillatory.
  \item \textbf{Flux expansion} (threshold $\theta$) adds neighbor $\boldsymbol{x}+\boldsymbol{\nu}_k$ only when reaction $k$ carries more than fraction $\theta$ of the total exit rate at $\boldsymbol{x}$.  By Corollary~\ref{cor:flux-expansion-bound}, $\Phi_{\mathrm{out}} \leq \theta\,\Phi_{\mathrm{total}}$, so the error bound tightens to $\theta\,\varepsilon_{\Delta t}$.  The threshold $\theta$ explicitly controls the size/accuracy tradeoff.
  \item \textbf{SSA expansion} samples one reaction direction per active state proportionally to propensities, adding the stochastically dominant neighbor.  This can be viewed as an implicit flux-reduction strategy: over successive steps the dominant-flux neighbors accumulate in $J$, driving $\Phi_{\mathrm{out}} \to 0$ in steady state without the deterministic $R$-fold state-space growth.  It is the appropriate choice for oscillatory systems where $\kappa_n < 1$ (no single dominant channel exists globally, but each state has a dominant local channel) and stoichiometric expansion would inflate $|J_n|$ to an unmanageable size.
\end{itemize}
All three strategies use the same time-step rule~\eqref{eq:flux-timestep}; the choice of strategy determines the realized $\Phi_{\mathrm{out}}/\Phi_{\mathrm{total}}$ ratio and hence the actual error-to-tolerance ratio.
\end{remark}

